Abstract
The separation of low concentration ethane $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{6})}$ from ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ is of great importance in petrochemical processes; however, it is widely considered to be a challenging task. Nonetheless, utilizing suitable $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ selective adsorbents, adsorptive approaches can be employed to obtain a series , namely $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Mn}$ , Zn, Cd), which were synthesized using N,N-di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8- naphthalenetetracarboxdiimide. Cooperative single component adsorption analysis, as well as (IAST) , Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulation revealed that higher adsorption capacity and preferential binding affinity for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ over $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ . These observations are due the presence of favorable pore environments in the MOFs, which result in strong $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ adsorption potential. The specific $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ binding sites were clearly determined by density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Thus the described M-PNMI materials can efficiently remove ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ 1 $1/9$ and 1/15, v/v) mixtures to directly produce high purity $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $(>99.99\%)$ under ambient conditions.
Keywords: Metal-organic frameworks, adsorptive separation, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ purification, density functional theory, breakthrough curves
## 1. Introduction
Ethylene $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4})$ is one of the most important feedstocks in petrochemical industries, with a global production of approximately 170 million tons in 2018. Within the petrochemical industry, ethylene is primarily obtained by steam cracking of naphtha and dehydrogenation of ethane $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6})$ [1, 2]. It is difficult to separate ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ due to the similar physical and chemical properties of the two components [3]. The separation and isolation of such feedstocks from hydrocarbon mixtures is traditionally carried out in distillation columns that are energy demanding [4, 5]. To obtain a polymer grade $\mathrm{{C_{2}H_{4}}}$ product with purity higher than $99.95\%$ , it needs to undergo a multi-stage and multi-step rectification purification process [6]. Therefore, to avoid such a high consumption of energy, more efficient separation technologies at ambient conditions are highly sought after. Among technologies combining low energy consumption and high selectivity, adsorption processes have shown remarkable performance [7-24]. Various selective adsorbents for this type of separations already exist and such approaches are expected to be commonly employed for the separation of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ from ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ . However, common adsorbents such as molecular sieves, modified activated carbon, or metal complexes exhibit poor adsorption selectivity for light hydrocarbon mixtures due to the lack of recognition mechanisms. Notably, preparation of the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}.$ - selective adsorbent and selective adsorption of the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ impurity can result in the production of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ satisfying the polymerization requirement in just one step [25-32]. Hence, it is urgent and necessary to develop efficient adsorbents for capturing ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures.
Recently, as multifunctional porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted significant attention in the areas of gases storage and separation due to the advantages of large specific surface area and adjustable pore structure and size, particularly for light hydrocarbons . Among the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ separation processes, utilizing ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ -selective MOFs is most favorable. Based on various separation mechanisms, such as the gate-opening effect, hydrogen bonding , and $\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}^{\cdots\pi}$ interaction , a number of ethane-selective MOFs have been described. The previously reported MOF materials, which preferentially adsorb ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}.$ , are the zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs). Gascon et al. demonstrated the first example of a microporous material, ZIF-7, for the efficient separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures via a gate-opening mechanism. However, as a consequence of the gate-opening pressures of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ being too close and due to the lack of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption sites in ZIF-7, lower separation efficiency is achieved utilizing this method. Subsequently, Li et al. also applied adsorption and dynamic breakthrough experiments to Ni(bdc)(ted)0.5 and PCN-250 . The group showed that both MOFs selectively absorb ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , relying on the synergy of the internal hole effect and the van der Waals force. In addition, in 2015, Chen et al. reported a microporous MOF, MAF-49, which efficiently removes C2H6 from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures . MAF-49 contains narrow 1D zigzag channels $(0.33\mathrm{nm}\times0.30\mathrm{nm})$ and various highly polar functional groups oriented preferentially towards $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ . These collaboratively exhibit high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity, based on stronger hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions. Nonetheless, because of the presence of small pore cavities, the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption capacity of this material is relatively low (approx. $38~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ . Furthermore, in 2018, our group reported the ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}(\mathbf{O}_{2})$ (dobdc)  material, which was demonstrated to display benchmark $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity and good $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures separation performance. However, strong interactions between the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecule and the active iron-peroxo sites resulted in high adsorption heat $\mathrm{(Q_{st}~=~-66.8~\ k J/m o l)}$ , which requires increased energy consumption during the regeneration process. Decoration of the ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ preferential binding sites to ligands would allow for a significant reduction in the associated adsorption heat, but also the construction of a favorable pore environment for selective binding of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ .
