## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
The following materials were prepared according to a previously reported study Unless mentioned, the c grade chemicals were used as received without any further purification. Manganese(II) acetate tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(Mn(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ , AR) and Zinc nitrate hexahydrate (Zn(NO3)2·6H2O, AR) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Cadmium nitrate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , AR) was purchased from SigmaAldrich. N,N'-di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxdiimide (DPNI, ${>}98\%$ was purchased from Chemsoon Co. Ltd.
Synthesis of Mn-PNMI. A mixture of Mn(CH3COO)2·4H2O (148 mg, 0.6 mmol,) and DPNI $\mathrm{84.076~mg}$ , 0.2 mmol,) was dissolved in a 12:1:1 (v/v/v) mixture of DMF:ethanol:water (7 mL) $(\mathrm{DMF}=N,N $ -dimethylformamide). The solution was stirred for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to obtain a homogeneous solution and then transferred to a $23~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon vessel in an autoclave for synthesis at $383\mathrm{K}$ for 2 days. Following cooling of the sample to ambient temperature, a pale brown porous material was obtained as the desired product.
Synthesis of Zn-PNMI. A mixture of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (178.49 mg, 0.6 mmol) and DPNI (63.057 mg, 0.15 mmol) was dissolved in a 12:1:1 (v/v/v) mixture of DMF:ethanol:water $7~\mathrm{mL}$ ). With the exception of the reaction time $(36\mathrm{~h})$ , the following steps were analogous to above. Following cooling of the sample to ambient temperature, a pale orange porous material was obtained as the product.
Synthesis of Cd-PNMI. A mixture of Cd(NO3)2·4H2O (185.1 mg, 0.6 mmol) and DPNI (84.076 mg, 0.2 mmol) was dissolved in a 12:1:1 (v/v/v) mixture of DMF:ethanol:water $7\ \mathrm{mL}$ ). With the exception of the reaction time $(24\mathrm{~h})$ , the following steps were analogous to above. Following cooling of the sample to ambient temperature, a pale yellow porous material was obtained as the product.
Final treatment and activation. After decanting the supernatant, the assynthesized materials were washed twice with dry DMF $(99.9\%)$ , followed by exchanging with dry MeOH $99.9\%$ , three exchange cycles of $3{\cdot}6\mathrm{h}$ ). For each wash, the M-PNMI product was separated by centrifugation. The materials were dried at 373 K. The activation process was conducted under dynamic vacuum at $453\mathrm{K}$ over $4\mathrm{{h}}$ for Mn-PNMI and Zn-PNMI. Furthermore, Cd-PNMI was activated at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ over $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) results were consistent with the previously reported study . The activated materials were stored in a glove-box under dry argon.
## 2.2 Characterization
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out under an air atmosphere using a NETZSCH STA 449F5 thermal analyzer at a heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \operatorname*{min}^{-1}$ . PXRD patterns were measured on a Bruker D8 ADVANCE X-ray diffractometer employing Cu-Kα radiation $(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . The morphologies of the samples were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Japan Hitachi SU8010).
## 2.3 Equilibrium adsorption measurement
High-purity $\mathrm{{C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}_{4}}}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ were used for single-component adsorption measurements. The $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ sorption measurements were performed on an Intelligent Gravimetric Analyzer (IGA 001, Hiden, UK). $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms were measured on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 at $77\mathrm{K}$ .
## 2.4 Breakthrough separation experiments and procedures
The experimental equipment involves two gas columns (Fig. S4), one linked to a stainless steel column which has an inner dimension of $\$4\times120\:\mathrm{mm}$ , and the other emitted outdoors. The temperature of the adsorption bed was maintained at $298\mathrm{K}$ using a thermostat. The outlet and inlet flow and pressure of the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures were controlled by a mass flow meter and a pressure gauge, respectively. Outlet effluent from the adsorption column was regularly tested by gas chromatography (GC-2014C SHIMADZU) with a thermal conductivity detector (TCD). To ensure the phase purity of the sample, powder X-ray diffraction was performed after each test. For a typical cycle experiment, the sample was regenerated in a vacuum oven at $393\mathrm{K}$ for another 2 h and cooled to room temperature in a glovebox.
## 2.5 Methods and models used for the simulation
Grand Canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulation was first utilized to calculate the distribution of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture adsorption on Mn-PNMI. The LennardJones interactions were calculated with a cutoff radius of $12.0\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . In addition, all of the Lennard-Jones cross interaction parameters were determined using the LorentzBerthelot mixing rule. The MOF structure was treated as rigid with atoms frozen at their crystallographic positions during the GCMC simulations. For each state point, GCMC simulation consisted of $2.0\times10^{7}$ steps to guarantee equilibration.
Density-functional theory (DFT) calculations were subsequently performed to identify the interactions between the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecules and the framework. DFT calculations were conducted using the Quantum-Espresso package. A semi-empirical addition of dispersive forces to conventional DFT was included in the calculation to account for the van der Waals interactions. The structure of Mn-PNMI was first optimized, and subsequently the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ or ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ gas molecules were introduced to potential locations on the MOF (based on the GCMC calculation), followed by full structural relaxations.
