To further explore the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption properties of these materials, single component gas adsorption was measured for M-PNMI $\mathbf{M}{=}\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{n}}$ , Zn, Cd) at 298 K (Fig. 2a-c) and $273\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. S3) for pressures up to 1 bar. It is apparent that the adsorption capacity towards ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ was higher than towards $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ for the all of the studied materials both at 298 and 273 K. Furthermore, attributes to larger pore channels and smaller atomic mass (Fig. S2), Mn-PNMI exhibited the highest gravimetric adsorption capacity of $61.82~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ $(2.76\ \mathrm{mmol/g})$ for ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $45.25~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (2.02 $\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g})$ for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar among M-PNMI $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Zn, Cd), holding a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ uptake ratio of $136\%$ . This outcome was higher than some of the benchmark materials including $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) $(130\%)$ and MUF-15 ( $(113\%)$ (Fig. 2d). When the temperature decreased to $273\mathrm{K}$ , the uptake of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ was also higher than that of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ for the three investigated M-PNMI MOFs. Mn-PNMI was shown to display the highest ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ adsorption capacity of up to $4.11\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 bar (Fig. S3a). The preferential binding of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ showed the remarkable potential of these materials for the removal of low concentration of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from C2H6/C2H4 mixtures.
To predict the separation performance of M-PNMI (M=Mn, Zn, Cd) for removal of the ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ impurity from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures, the Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory (IAST) was applied to calculate the IAST selectivity for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (1/9 and 1/15, v/v) mixtures (Fig. 3a,3c). The selectivity of M-PNMI $\mathbf{M}{=}\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{n}}$ , Zn, Cd) was determined to be 1.38 for Mn-PNMI, 1.42 for Zn-PNMI, and 1.27 for Cd-PNMI at 1 bar. Furthermore, the adsorption heat was calculated to assess the adsorption interaction between the MPNMI (M=Mn, Zn, Cd) materials and gas molecules. As for the equilibrium separations, the interaction differences between the individual guest molecules and the host structure are the key factors determining the separation performance. As expected, M-PNMI 1 $\mathbf{M}{=}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Zn, Cd) exhibited flat adsorption curves with increasing pressure and relative low adsorption heat compared to other MOFs (Fig. 2c and 3d). This avoided the particularly high adsorption heat and energy consumption in the regeneration process.
